ical-model-and-machine-learning">Difference between Statistical Model and Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-ranking-algorithms-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Ranking Algorithms in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/multicollinearity-causes-effects-and-detection">Multicollinearity: Causes, Effects and Detection</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-n-grams-model">Bag of N-Grams Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tf-idf-model">TF-IDF Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-probabilistic-training-data-sampling-methods-in-machine-learning">5 Probabilistic Training Data Sampling Methods in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-websites-to-download-pre-trained-machine-learning-models">5 Websites to Download Pre-trained Machine Learning Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/a-comprehensive-comparison-of-ml-experiment-tracking-tools">A Comprehensive Comparison of ML Experiment Tracking Tools</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/adam-optimizer">Adam Optimizer</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solution-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solution in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solutions-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solutions in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/arithmetic-geometric-and-harmonic-mean">Arithmetic Geometric and Harmonic Mean</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/assumptions-of-logistic-regression">Assumptions of Logistic Regression</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/attention-mechanism-in-machine-learning">Attention Mechanism in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/autoencoder-classification-in-keras">Autoencoder Classification in Keras</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/automating-api-testing-with-postman">Automating API Testing With Postman</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-words-bow-model-in-machine-learning">Bag of Words (BoW) Model in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/batch-normalization">Batch Normalization</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-books-for-natural-language-processing-with-python">Best Books for Natural Language Processing With Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-computer-vision-books">Best Computer Vision Books</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bleu-score-in-machine-learning">Bleu Score in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/building-a-machine-learning-regression-model-from-a-multivariate-nonlinear-dataset">Building a Machine Learning Regression Model from a Multivariate Nonlinear Dataset</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/complex-multi-panel-figures">Complex Multi Panel Figures</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conformal-prediction-for-machine-learning-classification">Conformal Prediction for Machine Learning Classification</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-types-in-statistics-used-for-machine-learning">Data Types in Statistics Used for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/demographic-based-recommender-systems">Demographic-Based Recommender Systems</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/differences-between-machine-learning-engineers-and-researchers">Differences Between Machine Learning Engineers And Researchers</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/distance-measures-for-machine-learning">Distance Measures for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/early-stopping-in-machine-learning">Early Stopping in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/f2-score">F2 Score</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/gradient-boosting-algorithm">Gradient Boosting Algorithm</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-do-you-increase-statistical-power">How do you increase Statistical power</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-softmax-activation-function-works">How Softmax Activation Function Works</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-calculate-correlation-between-variables-in-python">How to Calculate Correlation Between Variables in Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-causality-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Causality in Machine Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading4"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse4" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse4" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading4" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse4"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-science">Data Science Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/nlp">NLP Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading5"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse5" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse5" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading5" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse5"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/machine-learning-interview-questions">Top 50 Machine learning Interview Questions and Answers (2025)</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="dog-breed-classification-using-transfer-learning">next →</a> <a class="next" href="resnet-residual-network">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Image Caption Generation using Deep Learning</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>19 Jul 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 14 min read</span></div><p>The task of generating captions of images is a complicated one that involves natural language processing along with computer vision. This paper will discuss the steps of developing an image caption generator that can analyze the context of an image and narrate it in terms of natural language like English.</p><p>We are going to utilize Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) that will extract the images and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to compile useful sentences. To be able to train, we will use Flickr8K, which consists of 8,000 distinct photos. On Kaggle, this data is downloadable.</p><h3 class="h3">Step 1: Importing the Required Libraries</h3><p>Here, we will undergo the process of importing all the necessary libraries that we will be required for our project. These are <a href="numpy-tutorial">NumPy</a>, <a href="python-pandas">Pandas</a>, and OS data handling, Seaborn visualization, TensorFlow development of deep learning models, PIL processing and manipulation of image-related functions and Scikit-learn, utility functions and evaluation.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">import os
import warnings
warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd
import seaborn as sns
import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow.keras.applications.inception_v3 import InceptionV3
from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.image import img_to_array, load_img
from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.text import Tokenizer
from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.sequence import pad_sequences
from tensorflow.keras.utils import to_categorical, plot_model
from tensorflow.keras.models import Model, load_model
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Input, Dense, LSTM, Embedding, add
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Flatten, Dropout, BatchNormalization
from tensorflow.keras. optimizers import Adam
from tensorflow.keras.callbacks import EarlyStopping, LearningRateScheduler
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from PIL import Image
from tqdm import tqdm_notebook
from collections import Counter
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 2: Loading the Descriptions</h3><p>The file with captions that are assigned to the images is formatted so that each one of its lines looks like this: image filename space image description new line (\n). These are in the CSV format. In order to work with it effectively, we will form a dictionary, the names of the images, in which the list of corresponding descriptions is matched.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">images_directory = '/kaggle/input/flickr8k/Images/'
captions_path = '/kaggle/input/flickr8k/captions.txt'
def load_captions(file_path):
    with open(file_path, 'r') as f:
        captions = f.readlines()
        captions = [caption.lower() for caption in captions[1:]]
    return captions

def tokenize_captions(captions):
    tokenizer = Tokenizer()
    tokenizer.fit_on_texts(captions)
    return tokenizer

captions = load_captions(captions_path)
captions[:15:3]
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 3: Clean up the text</h3><p>One of the most important concerns of natural language processing is text cleaning by eliminating chaos so that the model can be able to pick up trends. Noise is usually of special characters, punctuation, numbers, and hashtags and may lead to confusion of the model. Through this elimination, we enhance the information that we have. We may also use the <a href="natural-language-toolkit">NLTK</a> library to eliminate stop words and stem or lemmatize a text to do additional text normalization.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def clean_text(text):
    text = re.sub(r'[^\w\s]', '', text)
    text = re.sub(r'\d+', '', text)
    text = re.sub(r'\s+', ' ', text).strip()
    return text
    
cleaned_captions = [clean_text(caption.split(',')[1]) for caption in captions]
cleaned_captions[:15:2]
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 4: Clearing the Captions</h3><p>In order to read the captions to be trained as our model, we implement additional processing on the captions by inserting special tokens at the start of each sentence as well as at the end. Such start and end tokens assist the model in knowing the beginning and end of a caption, which is very crucial in proper sequence generation.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">captions_IDs = []
for i in range(len(cleaned_captions)):
    item = captions[i].split(',')[0]+'\t'+'start '+cleaned_captions[i]+' end\n'
    captions_IDs.append(item)

captions_IDs[:20:3], len(captions_IDs)
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 5: Upload sample pictures with captions</h3><p>The training images here are connected with their descriptions acquired in the descriptions variable. In the first step, we make a list of filenames of all the training images. We can then construct an empty dictionary and each image file name to its list of captions. During mapping, we also append special start- and end-tokens to every caption to define the start and end of the sentence very precisely; this assists the model in learning how to structure sentences correctly.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def visualaization(data, num_of_images):
    captions_dictionary = {}
    for item in data[100:100+(num_of_images)*5]:
        image_id, caption = item.split('\t')
        if image_id not in captions_dictionary:
            captions_dictionary[image_id] = []
        captions_dictionary[image_id].append(caption)
    else:
        list_captions = [x for x in captions_dictionary.items()]

    count = 1
    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(10,20))
    for filename in list(captions_dictionary.keys()):
        captions = captions_dictionary[filename]
        image_load = load_img(images_directory+filename, target_size=(199,199,3))

        ax = fig.add_subplot(num_of_images,2,count,xticks=[],yticks=[])
        ax.imshow(image_load)
        count += 1

        ax = fig.add_subplot(num_of_images,2,count)
        plt.axis('off')
        ax.plot()
        ax.set_xlim(0,1)
        ax.set_ylim(0,len(captions))
        for i, caption in enumerate(captions):
            ax.text(0,i,caption,fontsize=20)
        count += 1
    plt.show()

visualaization(captions_IDs, 5) 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 6: Going through caption length distribution</h3><p>We look at the length of the captions as they determine the number of words they usually hold. This is the analysis that shall guide us in arriving at the right value of maximum sequence length in order to train the model.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def captions_length(data):
    plt.figure(figsize=(15, 7), dpi=300)
    sns.set_style('darkgrid')
    sns.histplot(x=[len(x.split(' ')) for x in data], kde=True, binwidth=1)
    plt.title('Captions length histogram', fontsize=15, fontweight='bold')
    plt.xticks(fontweight='bold')
    plt.yticks(fontweight='bold')
    plt.xlabel('Length', fontweight='bold')
    plt.ylabel('Freaquency', fontweight='bold')
    plt.show()

captions_length(cleaned_captions)
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 7: Vocabulary tokenization</h3><p>The textual captions are converted to numerical sequences using the Tokenizer provided in <a href="keras">Keras</a>. This converts every single word into an integer, thereby simplifying the process of analyzing the data and learning from the model.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">tokenizer = tokenize_captions(cleaned_captions)
vocab_size = len(tokenizer.word_index) + 1
vocab_size
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 8: Dividing the Data into Train, Validation and Test Set</h3><p>We split the dataset into three: training, validation and testing sets. This assists us in training the model, determining hyperparameters, and testing its performance on out-of-sample data to see whether it works well.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">all_image_ids = os.listdir(images_directory)

train_image_ids, val_image_ids = train_test_split(all_image_ids, test_size=0.15, random_state=42)
val_image_ids, test_image_ids = train_test_split(val_image_ids, test_size=0.1, random_state=42)

train_captions, val_captions, test_captions = [], [], []
for caption in captions_IDs:
    image_id, _ = caption.split('\t')

    if image_id in train_image_ids:
        train_captions.append(caption)

    elif image_id in val_image_ids:
        val_captions.append(caption)

    elif image_id in test_image_ids:
        test_captions.append(caption)

    else:
        print('Unknown image ID !')

train_captions[0], val_captions[0], test_captions[0], len(train_captions)/5, len(val_captions)/5, len(test_captions)/5
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 9: Obtain Feature Vectors of Images</h3><p>Machines do not understand images in the sense of a human eye, so we must transform the images into a form understandable to machines: the feature vectors. We can accomplish that by using transfer learning, which means that we use a model that has already learned useful patterns from large datasets. This time is the InceptionV3 model that has been trained by the <a href="what-is-imagenet-challenge-ilsvrc">ImageNet</a> dataset, and it has 1,000 categories of objects.</p><p>Instead, we import these model classes/applications / discard the final classification layer and have each of the images produce a 2048-dimensional feature vector. Our captioning model will be image-represented by these vectors.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def preprocess_image(image_path):
    img = load_img(image_path, target_size=(299, 299))
    img = img_to_array(img)
    img = np.expand_dims(img, axis=0)
    img = tf.keras.applications.inception_v3.preprocess_input(img)
    return img

def extract_image_features(model, image_path):
    img = preprocess_image(image_path)
    features = model.predict(img, verbose=0)
    return features

inception_v3_model = InceptionV3(weights = 'imagenet', input_shape=(299, 299, 3))
inception_v3_model.layers.pop()
inception_v3_model = Model(inputs=inception_v3_model.inputs, outputs=inception_v3_model.layers[-2].output)
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 10: Use Image Feature Extraction (Training, Validation and Testing)</h3><p>As a means of training the Image Caption Generator, we create feature vectors of all images with the help of the pre-trained InceptionV3 model. These vectors are put as input to the captioning model. We save the features that we extracted in each of the dictionaries of training, validation and test data and have each image associated with its feature representation.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">train_image_features, val_image_features, test_image_features = {}, {}, {}  # A Dictionary to store image features with their corresponding IDs

pbar = tqdm_notebook(total=len(all_image_ids), position=0, leave=True, colour='green')

for caption in all_image_ids:
    image_id = caption.split('\t')[0]
    image_path = os.path.join(images_directory, image_id)
    image_features = extract_image_features(inception_v3_model, image_path) # Extracting features

    if image_id in train_image_ids:
        train_image_features[image_id] = image_features.flatten()  # Flattening the features
        pbar.update(1)

    elif image_id in val_image_ids:
        val_image_features[image_id] = image_features.flatten()  # Flattening the features
        pbar.update(1)

    elif image_id in test_image_ids:
        test_image_features[image_id] = image_features.flatten()  # Flattening the features
        pbar.update(1)

    else:
        print('Unknown image ID !')

pbar.close() 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 11: How to Make a Data Generator that Trains a Model</h3><p>It is frequently advisable to train deep learning models on large datasets that do not fit in memory. To manage this more effectively, we came up with a data generator that produces batches of image features with their respective tokenized captions dynamically. The use of such a dynamic approach permits the model to work with data in smaller pieces, which significantly boosts memory and enables its scalable training.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def data_generator(captions, image_features, tokenizer, max_caption_length, batch_size):
    num_samples = len(captions)
    image_ids = list(image_features.keys())
    while True:
        np.random.shuffle(image_ids)  # Shuffle image_ids for each epoch
        for start_idx in range(0, num_samples, batch_size):
            end_idx = min(start_idx + batch_size, num_samples)
            X_images, X_captions, y = [], [], []
            for caption in captions[start_idx:end_idx]:
                image_id, caption_text = caption.split('\t')
                caption_text = caption_text.rstrip('\n')
                seq = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences([caption_text])[0] # Tokenizing the caption
                for i in range(1, len(seq)):
                    in_seq, out_seq = seq[:i], seq[i] # X_caption, Y
                    in_seq = pad_sequences([in_seq], maxlen=max_caption_length)[0]
                    out_seq = to_categorical([out_seq], num_classes=vocab_size)[0]
                    X_images.append(image_features[image_id])
                    X_captions.append(in_seq)
                    y.append(out_seq)

            yield [np.array(X_images), np.array(X_captions)], np.array(y)


max_caption_length = max(len(caption.split()) for caption in cleaned_captions) + 1

cnn_output_dim = inception_v3_model.output_shape[1] # 2048

batch_size_train = 270
batch_size_val = 150

train_data_generator = data_generator(train_captions, train_image_features, tokenizer, max_caption_length, batch_size_train)
val_data_generator = data_generator(val_captions, val_image_features, tokenizer, max_caption_length, batch_size_val)
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 12: Setting up the Image Captioning Model</h3><p>The built-in Keras Functional API is our choice to design our Image Captioning model. The main components of the architecture are the following:</p><p><strong>Image Feature Extractor (CNN output process)</strong></p><p>The convolutional model (InceptionV3) is then used to generate a 2048-dimensional feature vector which undergoes Batch Normalization. It is then fed into a Dense layer to compress it into a 256-dimensional vector, though this makes it match the <a href="lstm-autoencoder">LSTM</a> output size.</p><p><strong>Decoding (LSTM-based Decoder): Text Sequence Processor</strong></p><p>The tokenized captions are then injected into an Embedding layer, through which each word is represented as a dense vector that carries its semantics. These embeddings are further passed through an LSTM layer in order to learn long-term dependencies within the sequence.</p><p><strong>Final Prediction and Decoder</strong></p><p>The text and image processors provide two output streams, which are added together in an additional operation. The concatenated features are run through a Dense layer, which helps to fine-tune the features, and a last Dense layer with activation set to softmax, which helps to predict the next word in the caption sequence.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def build_model(vocab_size, max_caption_length, cnn_output_dim):
    input_image = Input(shape=(cnn_output_dim,), name='Features_Input')
    fe1 = BatchNormalization()(input_image)
    fe2 = Dense(256, activation='relu')(fe1) # Adding a Dense layer to the CNN output to match the decoder output size
    fe3 = BatchNormalization()(fe2)

    input_caption = Input(shape=(max_caption_length,), name='Sequence_Input')
    se1 = Embedding(vocab_size, 256, mask_zero=True)(input_caption)
    se2 = LSTM(256)(se1)

    decoder1 = add([fe3, se2])
    decoder2 = Dense(256, activation='relu')(decoder1)
    outputs = Dense(vocab_size, activation='softmax', name='Output_Layer')(decoder2)

    model = Model(inputs=[input_image, input_caption], outputs=outputs, name='Image_Captioning')
    return model

caption_model = build_model(vocab_size, max_caption_length, cnn_output_dim)

optimizer = Adam(learning_rate=0.01, clipnorm=1.0)
caption_model.compile(loss='categorical_crossentropy', optimizer=optimizer)

caption_model.summary() 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 13: Early Stopping and Learning Rate Scheduling as the Training Method of the Model</h3><p>We train the image caption model by the <a href="adam-optimizer">Adam optimizer</a> and cross-entropy loss calculating as categorical. With regard to overfitting, we use early stopping that quits on reaching a specific number of epochs because the validation loss fails to decrease.</p><p>We also have a learning rate scheduler that will help us slowly lower the learning rate with time towards convergence, stabilization, and efficiency. The model is trained to a maximum of 15 iterations, and the entire training process is monitored on the validation set.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">early_stopping = EarlyStopping(monitor='val_loss', patience=3, restore_best_weights=True)

def lr_scheduler(epoch, lr):
    return lr * tf.math.exp(-0.6)

lr_schedule = LearningRateScheduler(lr_scheduler)

history = caption_model.fit(train_data_generator, steps_per_epoch=len(train_captions) // batch_size_train,
                        validation_data=val_data_generator, validation_steps=len(val_captions) // batch_size_val,
                        epochs=15, callbacks=[early_stopping, lr_schedule])
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 14: Training and Validation Loss</h3><p>We add the training loss and the validation loss to a plot against the epoch to see how the model is learning. This visualization will guide us in the indication of overfitting and whether the model is performing better after every iteration to generalize effectively on new data.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">plt.figure(figsize=(15, 7), dpi=200)
sns.set_style('whitegrid')
plt.plot([x+1 for x in range(len(history.history['loss']))], history.history['loss'], color='#E74C3C', marker='o')
plt.plot([x+1 for x in range(len(history.history['loss']))], history.history['val_loss'], color='#641E16', marker='h')
plt.title('Train VS Validation', fontsize=15, fontweight='bold')
plt.xticks(fontweight='bold')
plt.yticks(fontweight='bold')
plt.xlabel('Epoch', fontweight='bold')
plt.ylabel('Loss', fontweight='bold')
plt.legend(['Train Loss', 'Validation Loss'], loc='best')
plt.show()
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 15: Greedy Search to Captions</h3><p>To obtain image captions, we apply the greedy search strategy, which anticipates one word at a time. The model passes through all steps one by one, choosing the word containing the highest predicted probability at each step, and completes a complete sentence until it reaches the end token.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def greedy_generator(image_features):
    in_text = 'start '
    for _ in range(max_caption_length):
        sequence = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences([in_text])[0]
        sequence = pad_sequences([sequence], maxlen=max_caption_length).reshape((1,max_caption_length))
        prediction = caption_model.predict([image_features.reshape(1,cnn_output_dim), sequence], verbose=0)
        idx = np.argmax(prediction)
        word = tokenizer.index_word[idx]
        in_text += ' ' + word
        if word == 'end':
            break

    in_text = in_text.replace('start ', '')
    in_text = in_text.replace(' end', '')

    return in_text 
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 16: Beam Search to create Captions</h3><p>To get better quality captions, we use beam search that, instead of only working with the most likely sequence of words, at each step considers many possible sequences. At every step, the algorithm stores the K most likely sequences so the algorithm can go through the better options and come up with more coherent and meaningful captions.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def beam_search_generator(image_features, K_beams = 3, log = False):
    start = [tokenizer.word_index['start']]
    start_word = [[start, 0.0]]
    for _ in range(max_caption_length):
        temp = []
        For s in start_word:
            sequence  = pad_sequences([s[0]], maxlen=max_caption_length).reshape((1,max_caption_length))
            preds = caption_model.predict([image_features.reshape(1,cnn_output_dim), sequence], verbose=0)
            word_preds = np.argsort(preds[0])[-K_beams:]
            for w in word_preds:
                next_cap, prob = s[0][:], s[1]
                next_cap.append(w)
                if log:
                    prob += np.log(preds[0][w]) # assign a probability to each K words
                else:
                    prob += preds[0][w]
                temp.append([next_cap, prob])
                
        start_word = temp
        start_word = sorted(start_word, reverse=False, key=lambda l: l[1])
        start_word = start_word[-K_beams:]

    start_word = start_word[-1][0]
    captions_ = [tokenizer.index_word[i] for i in start_word]
    final_caption = []
    for i in captions_:
        if i != 'end':
            final_caption.append(i)
        else:
            break
        
    final_caption = ' '.join(final_caption[1:])
    return final_caption
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 17: BLEU Score</h3><p>The BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy) score is a measurement that is famous for measuring the quality of the generated text, making the comparison with a single or multiple reference caption. It determines the similarity of the predicted captions to those written by humans and gives an objective evaluation of the performance of the model.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def BLEU_score(actual, greedy, beam_search):
    score_greedy_1 = corpus_bleu(actual, greedy, weights=(0.3, 0.3, 0.3, 0))
    score_greedy_2 = corpus_bleu(actual, greedy, weights=(0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25))
    score_BS_1 = corpus_bleu(actual, beam_search, weights=(0.3, 0.3, 0.3, 0))
    score_BS_2 = corpus_bleu(actual, beam_search, weights=(0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25))

    return [
        (f'BLEU-2 Greedy: {round(score_BS_2, 5)}'),
        (f'BLEU-1 Greedy: {round(score_BS_1, 5)}'),
        (f'Greedy: {greedy[0]}'),
        (f'BLEU-2 Beam Search: {round(score_greedy_2, 5)}'),
        (f'BLEU-1 Beam Search: {round(score_greedy_1, 5)}'),
        (f'Beam Search:  {beam_search[0]}')
    ]
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 18: The Process of Captions Creation on Test Pictures</h3><p>We form captioning sentences on the images of the test set by applying the greedy search method. A progress bar accompanies the process so that it is possible to observe the process of generation of captions in real mode as the model predicts one word after another on each picture.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">generated_captions = {}

pbar = tqdm_notebook(total=len(test_image_features), position=0, leave=True, colour='green')
for image_id in test_image_features:
    cap = greedy_generator(test_image_features[image_id])
    generated_captions[image_id] = cap
    pbar.update(1)

pbar.close()
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Step 19: Visualizing the Results of Caption Generation</h3><p>We select random images of the test set and show them with their real (ground truth) captions, together with the captions produced by greedy search and beam search. We also add BLEU scores of the generated captions to give a fair comparison of the results of the model with those of humans.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def visualization(data, greedy_caps, beamS_generator, evaluator, num_of_images):
    keys = list(data.keys()) 
    images = [np.random.choice(keys) for i in range(num_of_images)] # Randomly selected images
    count = 1
    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(6,20))
    for filename in images:
        actual_cap = data[filename]
        actual_cap = [x.replace("start ", "") for x in actual_cap]
        actual_cap = [x.replace(" end", "") for x in actual_cap]

        greedy_cap = greedy_caps[filename]
        beamS_cap = beamS_generator(test_image_features[filename])

        caps_with_score = evaluator(actual_cap, [greedy_cap]*(len(actual_cap)), [beamS_cap]*(len(actual_cap)))

        image_load = load_img(images_directory+filename, target_size=(199,199,3))
        ax = fig.add_subplot(num_of_images,2,count,xticks=[],yticks=[])
        ax.imshow(image_load)
        count += 1

        ax = fig.add_subplot(num_of_images,2,count)
        plt.axis('off')
        ax.plot()
        ax.set_xlim(0,1)
        ax.set_ylim(0,len(caps_with_score))
        for i, text in enumerate(caps_with_score):
            ax.text(0,i,text,fontsize=10)
        count += 1
    plt.show()

visualization(test_actual_captions, generated_captions, beam_search_generator, BLEU_score, 7)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre># Combined Output of Image Caption Generator (on Flickr8K)

